<p class="lead">
  iLovePDF is one of the most widely used PDF tool sites, with a genuinely broad feature set — but its free tier throttles how many operations you can run in a given stretch of time, which becomes the real bottleneck the moment you're processing more than a handful of files in one sitting.
</p>

<h2>If you want no rate limit and no account</h2>
<p>
  <a href="/">keptlocal</a> runs every operation in your browser instead of on a server, which is also why there's no hourly or daily cap to hit — there's no per-task server cost to ration on the free tier, because no server-side processing happens at all. 46 tools across PDF, image, and utility categories, all with no signup at any point.
</p>

<h2>If you specifically need OCR or format conversion to Word/Excel</h2>
<p>
  This is the case where iLovePDF's server-side processing is doing something a browser can't fully replicate yet. If your recurring need is OCR on scanned documents or converting PDFs into editable Word or Excel files with table extraction, iLovePDF (or a comparable service like Smallpdf) remains the more capable option for that specific task.
</p>

<h2>If you want a one-time desktop install</h2>
<p>
  <strong>PDF24 Creator</strong> is a free Windows desktop application bundling a large set of PDF tools that run locally once installed — worth considering if you'd rather install software once than depend on any website's rate limits or connectivity.
</p>

<h2>If you need professional-grade editing</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Adobe Acrobat</strong> remains the standard for advanced work — real redaction, building interactive forms, Bates numbering for legal filings. It's paid, but for professional workflows genuinely needing these specific features, it's built for exactly that.
</p>

